/** \page ssl SSL/TLS Communication

@section ssl_CertStore Certificate Management

SSL/TLS communication may require certificates and keys, i.e. for server or
client authentication and to build a list of trusted CA certificates to verify
the peer. Certificates and keys encoded in PKCS12 format can be loaded into
a into a Pt::Ssl::CertificateStore.

@code
Pt::Ssl::CertificateStore store;

const char* password = ...;
std::ifstream ifs("certs.p12");
store.loadPkcs12(ifs, password);

Pt::Ssl::CertificateStore::ConstIterator it;
for(it = store.begin(); it != store.end(); ++it)
{
    std::cout << "subject: " << it->subject() << std::endl;
}

const Pt::Ssl::Certificate* cert = store.findCertificate("SGC Mainframe");
if( ! cert)
    std::cerr << "certifictate is missing" << std::endl;
@endcode

The example above reads certificates and keys from a std::ifstream using an
user specified password. Certificates in the store can be inspected using the
iterator API, or searched for by a subject string. Note, that a private key
will be assigned to its certificate and is not exposed or accessible by any
API functions.

@section ssl_Context Context Initialization

Before any SSL/TLS connections can be established, a Pt::Ssl::Context has to
be created, which allows to use the same set of certificates and settings for
many connections.

@code
Pt::Ssl::Context ctx(Pt::Ssl::TLSv1);
ctx.setIdentity(myCert);
ctx.addCACertificate(myCA);
ctx.setVerifyMode(Pt::Ssl::AlwaysVerify);
@endcode

A context can be created for a specific protocol type, e.g. TLSv1 which limits
the communication to the TLSv1 protocol. The method setIdentity() sets the
certificate presented to the peer. If a private key is assigned to this
certificate, it will also be used. Any trusted CA certificates to verify the
peer's identity can be added using the addCACertificate() method. The verify
mode indicates whether the peer is required or to authenticate itself during
the handshake.

@section ssl_Connection Opening a Connection

A secure connection can be established using any std::iostream to transport
the encrypted data to the peer. The Pt::Ssl::IOStream only encodes and decodes
data. The certificates and settings from the context are used.

@code
std::iostream& ios = ...;

Pt::Ssl::IOStream ssl(ctx, ios, Pt::Ssl::Connect);
@endcode

When a Pt::Ssl::IOStream, or alternatively a Pt::Ssl::StreamBuffer is created
of opened, it is ready to perform the handshake. This can be done with the
methods readHandshake() and writeHandshake().

@code
Pt::Ssl::IOStream& ssl = ...;

while( ! ssl.isConnected() )
{
    bool wantRead = ssl.readHandshake();
    if(wantRead)
    {
        [ make more data available in the underlying stream ]

        continue;
    }
                
    bool wantWrite = ssl.writeHandshake();
    if( wantWrite )
    {
        [ flush the underlying stream ]

        continue;
    }
}
@endcode

Essentially, readHandshake() and writeHandshake() have to be called repeatedly
until the Pt::Ssl::IOStream is connected. If readHandshake() returns true we
need to continue reading, but not enough data was available in the underlying
stream. Only as many bytes will be consumed as std::iostream::in_avail()
indicates. This allows non-blocking communication, since readHandshake() can
be called again when more data becomes available. If readHandshake() returns
false, no more data needs to be read. Then writeHandshake() is called, which
returns true if data was written to the underlying stream that needs to be
transfered. In the non-blocking case, we can call writeHandshake() again once
the data was written.

Once the connection is open, data can be send or received from the peer. This
can be done using the blocking I/O API from std::iostream or the non-blocking
API offered by Pt::Ssl::IOStream and Pt::Ssl::StreamBuffer. In the non-blocking
case, available data can be imported from the underlying stream.

@code
Pt::Ssl::IOStream& ssl = ...;

for(;;)
{
    ssl.import();

    std::streamsize avail = ssl.sslBuffer().in_avail();
    if(avail <= 0)
        break;

    do
    {
        char buf[255];
        std::streamsize n = ssl.readsome( buf, sizeof(buf) );
        std::cout.write(buf, n);
    }
    while( ssl.sslBuffer().in_avail() > 0 );
}

if( ssl.isShutdown() )
{
    std::cout << "received shutdown alert" << std::endl;
}
@endcode

The method import() does not read more data from the underlying std::iostream
than in_avail() of it's std::streambuf indicates. It might read only a part
of the available data if not all bytes could be imported into the 
buffer of the Pt::Ssl::IOStream. To drain the underlying stream, import()
should be called in a loop. After calling import(), decrypted data may be
available and can be processed. It is also possible that a shutdown alert
was received, which can be checked for with isShutdown(). Premature EOF has
to be handled by the code that maintains the underlying std::iostream.

@section ssl_Shutdown Connection Shutdown

A connection shutdown alert can be initiated by either peer and the method
Pt::Ssl::IOStream::isShutdown() indicates that the connection is shutting 
down. If a shutdown alert is received during a read operation, 
Pt::Ssl::IOStream::shutdown() will write the shutdown acknowledge to the
underlying stream and return true. This data still needs to be sent to the peer
then, but from the perspective of the Pt::Ssl::IOStream, the connection is
considered closed and Pt::Ssl::IOStream::isShutdown() no longer returns true.
If the shutdown of a connection is initiated, a shutdown alert is written to
the underlying std::iostream and needs to be sent to the peer. When the
shutdown acknowledgement becomes available in the underlying std::iostream,
Pt::Ssl::IOStream::shutdown() must be called again to consume it, which will
return true if the shutdown acknowledge is complete.

*/